MEDIAPLAYER(1)                   User Commands                  MEDIAPLAYER(1)

NAME
       mediaplayer - play music, videos and internet streams

SYNOPSIS
       mediaplayer  [--fullscreen]  [--no-video]  [--shuffle]  [--repeat=mode]
       [--video-sink=name] [file|folder|playlist|URL...]
       mediaplayer --version

DESCRIPTION
       mediaplayer is the desktop's music and video player, the program behind
       LikeOS Media Player in the Programs menu and the one  a  double-clicked
       song or film opens in.  It plays whatever the installed GStreamer plug-
       ins  can decode: WAV, AIFF and AU sound, AAC and M4A, MP4, Matroska and
       WebM video with H.264, H.265, VP8 and VP9 pictures,  and  MP3,  Vorbis,
       Opus and FLAC where the FFmpeg decoders are enabled.  Streams play from
       http,  https and every other kind of address a GStreamer source element
       is installed for, including HLS and DASH.

       The window has a sidebar at the left: a search box and  the  places  in
       three  groups  --  the  library  (Recent, Music, Videos), playback (Now
       playing, Queue) and the playlists -- with Settings at  its  foot.   The
       page  it selects is shown beside it, and the transport strip runs along
       the bottom: what is playing at the left, the transport buttons over the
       seek bar in the middle, and the volume, the queue and the view  buttons
       at  the  right.  Sound goes through autoaudiosink, which picks the sys-
       tem's sound service, so the player mixes with everything else  that  is
       making a sound.

       Each  file,  folder,  playlist  or address given on the command line is
       added to the queue, and the first  one  starts  playing.   Folders  are
       searched  recursively  for  media files, which are queued in name order
       ("Track 2" before "Track 10"), each folder's files before its sub-fold-
       ers.  Hidden  files  and  folders  are  skipped.   M3U,  M3U8  and  PLS
       playlists are read and their entries queued; relative entries are taken
       relative  to  the  playlist, and paths with backslashes between folders
       are understood.

OPTIONS
       -f, --fullscreen
              Start in full screen.

       -a, --no-video
              Play the sound only.  Video streams  are  not  decoded  at  all,
              which saves the processor time a film's pictures would cost; the
              cover-art page is shown instead.

       -s, --shuffle
              Turn  shuffle  on.   Like the button, this is remembered for the
              next start.

       -r, --repeat=mode
              Set the repeat mode: none (or off), all (start the queue over at
              its end) or one (play the current entry again and  again).   Re-
              membered like the button.

       --video-sink=name
              Choose how pictures reach the screen, for this run only:

              auto    The default: the first of xv, gtk and x11 that works.

              xv      The  X  video extension.  The X server (and the graphics
                      hardware behind it) converts  and  scales  the  picture,
                      which  makes  it the cheapest by far.  Needs an Xv adap-
                      tor; xvinfo(1) lists them.

              gtk     Pictures drawn by GTK.   Works  on  every  display,  but
                      scales in software.

              x11     Plain  X  images.  Cannot scale, so the picture is shown
                      at its own size.

              gl      GTK's OpenGL area.  Never chosen automatically.

              If the chosen output cannot be opened, the  automatic  order  is
              tried.

       --version
              Print the program's and GStreamer's versions and exit.

       The standard GTK and GStreamer options are accepted as well (--display,
       --gst-debug-level, and so on); see mediaplayer --help-all.

THE WINDOW
       Recent Recently  played  music  and videos, and the albums added to the
              library last; buttons to open files, a folder or an internet ad-
              dress.

       Music  Every music file in the library folders (the user's Music folder
              unless others are chosen in the settings), as a list of songs or
              as tiles of albums and artists, chosen with  the  Songs,  Albums
              and  Artists switch.  Double-clicking a song plays the list from
              there; a tile opens the album or the artist, and its round  play
              button  plays it.  "Shuffle all" plays the whole library in ran-
              dom order.  Titles, artists and albums are read from the  files'
              tags once and kept in ~/.cache/mediaplayer/library; only new and
              changed files are read again.

       Videos The videos in the library folders (the Videos folder unless oth-
              ers  are  chosen),  as  tiles  with a picture from each, kept in
              ~/.cache/mediaplayer/thumbnails.

       Search Typing in the search box lists the library entries whose  title,
              artist or album contains the text.

       Playlists
              "New  playlist"  (or  the  +  beside the Playlists header in the
              sidebar) saves what is in the queue under a name;  a  playlist's
              tile plays it, and its right-button menu adds it to the queue or
              deletes    it.     They   are   kept   in   ~/.local/share/medi-
              aplayer/playlists.

       Now playing
              Clicking the cover in the transport strip (or Now playing in the
              sidebar) shows the cover large, over a wash of its  colours.   A
              video  comes  up  there by itself.  While a video plays, in full
              screen and in the mini player, the window turns  black  and  the
              dark  strips  above  and  below the picture slide away while the
              pointer rests.

       Seek bar
              Drag it, click it, or scroll on it.  While  it  is  dragged  the
              picture  follows  in  key-frame  steps;  the  final  position is
              reached exactly when it is let go.  Clicking  the  time  at  its
              right  switches between the length of the entry and the time re-
              maining.

       Transport
              Shuffle, previous, play/pause, next and repeat (stop is  in  the
              "More"  menu).   Previous  restarts  the current entry when more
              than three seconds of it have played, and goes to the entry  be-
              fore  otherwise.   The repeat button cycles through off, all and
              "this one".

       Volume The slider beside the speaker sets the volume; the speaker  but-
              ton  mutes and unmutes, and the scroll wheel over it changes the
              volume directly.  The slider follows the way loudness  is  heard
              (a  cubic  scale),  so its middle sounds like half.  It controls
              this program's stream only; the system  volume  is  audioctl(1).
              Turning the volume up also unmutes.

       Queue  Double-click an entry, or select it and press Enter, to play it.
              Entries  are  reordered  by dragging them, or with "Move up" and
              "Move down" in the right-button menu;  Delete  removes  the  se-
              lected ones.  Files and folders dropped on the list are inserted
              where  they  fall;  dropped  on  the  picture they are added and
              played at once.  Titles, artists and  lengths  of  queued  local
              files  are  read  in the background before they play.  The right
              mouse button opens a menu with the same actions.

       More   The "..." button at the  right  of  the  transport  strip:  open
              files,  folders,  locations  and  playlists; save the queue as a
              playlist; choose the audio track and the  subtitles  of  a  film
              with  several; set the playback speed (the pitch is kept); stop;
              the queue, the mini player and the settings; the media  informa-
              tion window, the keyboard shortcuts and the version.

       Mini player
              A  small  window  above the others with the cover and the trans-
              port, from its button in the transport strip or Ctrl+M;  Esc  or
              the same button brings the whole window back.

       Full screen
              Double-click  the  picture,  press  F or F11, or use the button.
              The transport strip slides away  while  the  pointer  rests  and
              comes back when it moves; Esc leaves full screen.

       The  middle  mouse button on the picture plays or pauses, the right one
       opens the menu, and the scroll wheel changes the volume.

KEYBOARD
       Space                 Play or pause.

       N, P                  Next  or  previous  entry  (also  Ctrl+Right  and
                             Ctrl+Left, and the media keys).

       Left, Right           Back  or  forward  5  seconds;  with  Shift,  one
                             minute.

       Home                  Back to the beginning.

       0 ... 9               Jump to 0 %, 10 % ... 90 % of the entry.

       Up, Down              Volume up or down by 5 %; also + and -.

       M                     Mute or unmute.

       S                     Shuffle on or off.

       R                     Repeat: off, all, this one.

       [, ]                  Slower or faster (0.5x to 2x); Backspace  returns
                             to normal speed.

       F, F11                Full screen on or off.

       Esc                   Leave  full  screen  or the mini player; from the
                             now-playing page or an album, go back.

       T                     Show the length or the time remaining.

       I, Ctrl+I             Media information: tags,  codecs,  picture  size,
                             sample  rate,  and  which video and sound outputs
                             are in use.

       F9, Q                 Show the queue, or go back from it.

       Ctrl+M                The mini player.

       Ctrl+F                Search the library.

       Delete                Remove the  selected  queue  entries  (while  the
                             queue has the keyboard).

       Ctrl+O                Open files; Ctrl+Shift+O opens a folder.

       Ctrl+L                Open a location (an internet address).

       Ctrl+S                Save the queue as a playlist.

       F1                    The list of shortcuts.

       Ctrl+Q                Quit.

       While  the queue has the keyboard (after it is clicked), the arrow keys
       move through it; clicking the picture gives them back  to  seeking  and
       the volume.

ERRORS
       An  entry that cannot be played is marked in the queue and explained in
       a dialog,  with  the  technical  details  folded  away  beneath.   When
       GStreamer  reports  that  it needs a plug-in that is not installed, the
       dialog names it -- "MPEG-1 Layer 3 (MP3) decoder", for example.  A film
       whose pictures play but whose sound (or subtitles)  cannot  be  decoded
       keeps playing, with a note above the picture.

       When  the queue moves on by itself and the next entry fails, that entry
       is skipped with a note instead of stopping everything behind a  dialog,
       unless every entry in turn has failed.

FILES
       ~/.config/mediaplayer/settings
              What  is  remembered  between runs: volume and mute, shuffle and
              repeat, the window size, whether  the  sidebar  is  folded,  the
              folder  the file chooser last opened, and the keys below.  A key
              file with one [Player] group, written with mode 0600.  Keys that
              are missing, mistyped or out of range fall  back  to  their  de-
              faults one by one.

              video-sink          The default for --video-sink.

              gapless             true  (the  default)  to run consecutive en-
                                  tries together without a pause.

              discover-metadata   false to stop titles and lengths  of  queued
                                  files from being read ahead.

              subtitles           false to start with subtitles hidden.

              music-folders, video-folders
                                  The  library  folders,  separated  by  semi-
                                  colons.

       ~/.config/mediaplayer/recent
              The recently played entries the Recent page shows.

       /usr/share/applications/mediaplayer.desktop
              The desktop entry: the applications menu, and the file manager's
              "Open With" list.

ENVIRONMENT
       MEDIAPLAYER_VIDEO_SINK
              Used instead of the remembered video-sink when  no  --video-sink
              is given.

       GST_DEBUG
              GStreamer's own diagnostics, as for every GStreamer program.

EXIT STATUS
       0      The window was closed.

       1      The  display  could  not be opened, or GStreamer (or its playbin
              element) is missing.

       2      A bad option.

SEE ALSO
       audioplay(1), audioctl(1), eventsound(1), xvinfo(1)

